.TH CB 1
.SH NAME
cb \- C program beautifier
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cb
[
.B -sj
] [
.B -l
.I n
] [
.I file
\&...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cb
is a real, structural translation of Plan 9's own
.IR cb (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/cb/{cb.c,cb.h,cbtype.c,cbtype.h} ,
1259 real C lines total - an earlier scoping estimate in
.B doc/plan9-port.md
had this at 1,061; both were wrong, see BUGS), compiled to Dis and run
by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
With no arguments,
.I cb
reformats standard input to standard output, reindenting C source
according to Plan 9's own house indentation conventions - one tab per
brace level, operators and keywords set off with the spacing this
tree's own
.B cb.h
tables specify, comments and string/character literals passed through
byte-for-byte. Given one or more
.IR file s,
each is reformatted in turn, its own output written to standard
output (not back to the file).
.TP
.B -s
Strict mode: a top-level declaration whose brace would otherwise sit
at tab stop 0 is instead placed at tab stop 1, matching the C source's
own
.B strict
flag exactly.
.TP
.B -j
Join mode: an
.B else
that would otherwise start its own line is joined onto the closing
.B }
of the preceding
.B if
block's braces instead.
.TP
.B -l
.I n
Sets the target line width used for the comment-folding heuristic
(default 2048, effectively unlimited) - a comment line longer than
.I n
columns is folded, with a
.B "/* code folded from here */"
marker inserted. Bundled
.RI ( -l 40)
and separate
.RI ( -l\ 40)
forms are both accepted, matching the C source's own
.B ARGBEGIN
bundling behavior.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/cb.b
(translated from
.BR cb.c / cb.h / cbtype.c / cbtype.h ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cbtest (1)
.SH BUGS
This tree's own
.B doc/plan9-port.md
scoping pass, done before any code was written, estimated the combined
translation at roughly 1,061 real C lines; a direct count of the four
source files put it at 1,259. Both the scoping estimate and (in the
other direction) a separate later projection of 1,850-1,950 Limbo
lines were wrong - the actual translation came out to about 1,650
Limbo lines. Recorded here because this is now the third line-count
estimate in this backlog's own documentation to miss, in both
directions; the lesson is that the ratio isn't predictive, not which
way it tends to be off.
.PP
.I Cb.b
deliberately does NOT use plan9port's own prebuilt
.B cb
binary as a test oracle, even though one exists on this host: it has
genuinely diverged from 9front's own source in at least three ways -
split continuation lines are indented with four spaces instead of a
tab, the
.B ::
(Icon-style generator) case is removed entirely, and its own
.B ARGBEGIN
bundled-flag parsing is silently broken for at least one flag
combination. Using it as an oracle would fail
.I cbtest (1)'s
cases for reasons that are plan9port's own divergence, not this port's
- the oracle is built from the actual 9front source instead (see
.IR cbtest (1)).
.PP
The C source's own
.B cbtype.c
table is indexed
.B (_cbtype_+1)[c]
for
.B c
ranging over
.B -1..127
only (EOF, matching C's own
.B int
promotion of a
.B char
argument, deliberately selects the table's leading zero entry at index
0). A byte with the high bit set - anything in
.B 128..255
- indexes past the C table's real 129 entries, which is undefined
behavior in C but would be a hard bounds-check exception in Limbo.
This translation sizes the table to 257 entries instead of 129 (valid
for
.B c
in
.BR -1..255 ,
with the added entries defaulting to zero) so that a high-bit byte is
DEFINED to behave as an ordinary, non-special character rather than
faulting - a deliberate divergence from the C source's own undefined
behavior, not a fidelity gap, and stated here plainly rather than left
implicit.
.PP
The C source's own
.B main()
never resets any of
.BR cb.h 's
global state between multiple
.I file
arguments on one command line - each file after the first inherits
whatever
.BR clevidx / dolevel / opflag /
etc. its predecessor left behind. This translation preserves that
behavior exactly in its own
.B init()
(the real CLI entry point's multi-file loop); it is the in-process
test seam's own
.B run()
that resets all such state at the top of every call, via a dedicated
.B resetstate()
not present in the C source at all - needed because
.I cbtest (1)
calls
.B run()
repeatedly in one process, unlike a real invocation's single pass.
Multi-file-argument invocation itself is not exercised by
.IR cbtest (1)
(see its own BUGS) - only the single-input seam is.
.PP
Translating
.BR lookup() 's
own C signature
.RB ( "char *first, char *last" ,
addressing either of two conceptually distinct buffers depending on
call site) surfaced a real bug in this translation's first pass, found
by testing real files and diffing output, not by re-reading the code -
see
.IR cbtest (1)'s
own BUGS for the full account.
